WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.029 --> 00:00:01.753
(brisk string music)

00:00:01.753 --> 00:00:04.392
- [Woman] Walking down this
quiet street in Brooklyn,

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:06.634
you'd never know that
inside this building,

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:10.002
within this tiny apartment, music is made.

00:00:10.002 --> 00:00:12.741
♪ Nobody reads from the Book of Job ♪

00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:16.709
♪ At the church where
me and my grandma go ♪

00:00:16.709 --> 00:00:19.635
♪ Nobody sees the trouble I know ♪

00:00:19.635 --> 00:00:23.661
♪ But I know that
trouble's gonna find me ♪

00:00:23.661 --> 00:00:25.954
- [Woman] Meet Evan and Luke.

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:28.105
They're longtime friends and roommates.

00:00:28.105 --> 00:00:30.899
- Evan and I have been
living together here for,

00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:32.677
how many years have we
been living together, man?

00:00:32.677 --> 00:00:33.560
- [Evan] About a year and a half?

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.754
- A year and a half?

00:00:34.754 --> 00:00:37.875
- [Woman] And once a month,
in only about 500 square feet,

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:40.510
they manage to pile in up to 60 musicians

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.107
for the ultimate recording session.

00:00:43.107 --> 00:00:45.934
- I think we should put five
or six people in the bathroom.

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.207
(cello scales)

00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:51.090
- For each arrangement, we
record it in about three hours,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.565
and post it online.

00:00:52.565 --> 00:00:55.059
(percussion rhythm)

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:56.658
- [Luke] A lot of people's
reactions are that

00:00:56.658 --> 00:00:58.517
it seems crazy, and it is crazy.

00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:01.067
- [Woman] To pull this off,
first, they have to uproot

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.280
their humble abode.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.404
- Anything that is not involved
in making music has to go.

00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:07.464
(drill whirring)

00:01:07.464 --> 00:01:08.998
- [Luke] We take all the furniture out,

00:01:08.998 --> 00:01:10.567
we take most of the doors off,

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.019
it's just all about making as much room

00:01:12.019 --> 00:01:13.434
and as much sight lines as possible.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.129
- [Evan] It really just looks like chaos;

00:01:15.129 --> 00:01:17.431
there's mic stands everywhere;

00:01:17.431 --> 00:01:21.187
the floor is littered with music stands.

00:01:21.187 --> 00:01:22.660
- I'm a little worried about this, though.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.288
- [Evan] There's not a
single inch to spare.

00:01:24.288 --> 00:01:26.980
- [Woman] And that's because
they make way for the strings,

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.870
woodwinds, horns, percussion,
and a full chorus.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:31.967
♪ Find me ♪

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:34.200
Yeah, that's a marimba in the kitchen.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.129
(marimba notes)

00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:36.154
But, why?

00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:37.105
- [Evan] I wanted an opportunity

00:01:37.105 --> 00:01:40.040
to record a bunch of
people at the same time.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.549
Luke wanted an opportunity to
hear his arrangements played,

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:45.964
so we just invited a bunch of people over.

00:01:45.964 --> 00:01:47.681
- [Woman] And although
it seems unconventional,

00:01:47.681 --> 00:01:49.391
there's a reason for the madness.

00:01:49.391 --> 00:01:50.574
- [Evan] We're trying to change the way

00:01:50.574 --> 00:01:53.521
that you might see an orchestra play.

00:01:53.521 --> 00:01:57.547
There is a special dynamic
and a feeling in the room that

00:01:57.547 --> 00:02:02.547
you won't get in a recording
studio or in a public venue.

00:02:02.727 --> 00:02:06.599
(wind instruments playing and clapping)

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:09.030
- [Woman] It's this unique
sound and sense of community

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.227
that has attracted musicians

00:02:10.227 --> 00:02:13.252
from all over the country to participate.

00:02:13.252 --> 00:02:14.251
- [Luke] There's definitely
something really exciting

00:02:14.251 --> 00:02:17.309
about the idea that this
really shouldn't be possible.

00:02:17.309 --> 00:02:19.204
- [Evan] It's a really unique experience,

00:02:19.204 --> 00:02:20.776
and I think that people are drawn to that.

00:02:20.776 --> 00:02:22.264
- [Woman] And so, they keep at it.

00:02:22.264 --> 00:02:24.405
♪ It'll find me ♪

00:02:24.405 --> 00:02:25.238
♪ Find me ♪

00:02:25.238 --> 00:02:28.041
- [Luke] It's a real kind of
jarring, wonderful experience

00:02:28.041 --> 00:02:29.449
every time and we love it.

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.915
- [Woman] And I bet the
neighbors must love it, too.

00:02:31.915 --> 00:02:35.017
- They're lucky they're good.

00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:37.267
- Hi! Hello, New York City!

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.642
(group conversing)

00:02:44.186 --> 00:02:46.186
(ding)

